# Test: `ze cli -c` reads the OSPFv3 views of every running address family
# (spec-ospfv3-views-every-address-family AC-9, AC-10).
#
# VALIDATES: with ipv6-unicast (Instance ID 0) and ipv4-unicast (Instance ID 64)
# configured, `ze cli -c "show ospf ipv6 database address-family ipv4-unicast | json"`
# answers the one ipv4-unicast record tagged with its instance-id and holding the LSA
# this router (10.0.0.1) advertised, the unnarrowed `| json` answers a tagged record
# for each family and no other, and `| table` shows the address-family and instance-id
# columns, in that order, for both.
# PREVENTS: the per-family rows reaching the plugin-dispatch route only
# (ospf-multiaf-views.ci) while the client an operator's script runs answers
# something else: the client speaks SSH, and the daemon's session applies the pipe
# chain against the OSPF plugin's declared answer shapes.
# WHY A .ci: the question is the whole client path, `ze cli` over SSH to the daemon,
# the builtin proxy, the plugin and the session's pipe renderer; the unit tests
# judge the producer and the renderer alone.
#
# Each `ze cli -c` is a runner step (policy-interface-list-counters.ci's shape), so the
# runner starts it the way it starts the daemon: under the netns launch mode both run
# as ZE_TEST_UID, the owner of the store the client opens, as an operator on the box
# does. A compiled fixture stays root there, and the store refuses a client it starts.
#
# The steps run once, so seq=2 is a barrier on the state they judge
# (ospfMultiAFViewsCLIReady, internal/test/fixture/register_ospf_multiaf_views_cli.go):
# the daemon's readiness file is written before the SSH listener accepts, and the
# engines originate their LSAs after startup. It waits, over SSH in its own process,
# until the database view holds a tagged record for each family, the ipv4-unicast one
# with an LSA 10.0.0.1 originated. No sleep stands in for it, since a fixed delay
# cannot know when an LSA is originated.
#
# `| json` renders the answer indented by two spaces with sorted keys
# (applyJSON, internal/component/command/pipe.go), so a record opens on a line of its
# own as `  {`, and its address-family is followed by its instance-id.
#
# `ze cli` speaks SSH and nothing else (internal/component/cli/client/main.go), so
# the daemon declares a server and a user, and the client takes its name and
# password from the environment (resolveUsername,
# internal/component/cli/sshclient/client.go); ze.ssh.insecure skips the host-key
# check the zefs store would otherwise answer.

# The daemon opens raw IPv6 sockets to run the OSPFv3 engines; darwin lacks that path, so
# this runs under the Linux/QEMU functional harness.
option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin
# The `ze -` test that serves `ze cli` over SSH in the netns selection
# (policy-interface-list-counters.ci) runs on disk storage; this one follows it.
option=storage:value=disk
# OSPFv3 opens raw proto-89 sockets on eth0/eth1 (IPv6 link-local, auto on link-up);
# provision the dummy links inside the per-test netns.
option=netns-link:name=eth0
option=netns-link:name=eth1:address=10.0.2.1/24
option=timeout:value=60s

stdin=ze-conf:terminator=EOF_CONF
ospf {
	router-id 10.0.0.1
	address-family {
		ipv6-unicast {
			instance-id 0
			areas {
				area 0.0.0.0 {
					area-type normal
				}
			}
			interfaces {
				interface eth0 {
					area 0.0.0.0
					network-type point-to-point
				}
			}
		}
		ipv4-unicast {
			instance-id 64
			areas {
				area 0.0.0.0 {
					area-type normal
				}
			}
			interfaces {
				interface eth1 {
					area 0.0.0.0
					network-type point-to-point
				}
			}
		}
	}
}
system {
	authentication {
		user operator {
			# bcrypt hash of testpass.
			password "$2a$04$UlwuiuH82Unfsq.XEMPGJeDkXwbm3KW.nvVaVXOd/JeFK8VjMjrQO";
		}
	}
}
environment {
	ssh {
		enabled true;
		server main {
			ip 127.0.0.1;
			port $PORT;
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

option=env:var=ze.ssh.username:value=operator
option=env:var=ze.ssh.password:value=testpass
option=env:var=ze.ssh.insecure:value=true
option=env:var=ze.ssh.port:value=$PORT

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-conf
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=le test fixture ospf/ospf-multiaf-views-cli:timeout=45s:exit=0
expect=stdout:contains=READY: the daemon's SSH session answers a tagged database record for each family

# AC-9: the narrowed view answers one record, ipv4-unicast at instance-id 64, holding
# this router's LSA. The reject on a second `  {` line refuses any second record, and
# the one on ipv6-unicast refuses the other family leaking through the narrowing.
cmd=foreground:seq=3:exec=ze cli -c "show ospf ipv6 database address-family ipv4-unicast | json":exit=0
expect=stdout:pattern="address-family": "ipv4-unicast",\s*"instance-id": 64,
expect=stdout:contains="advertising-router": "10.0.0.1"
reject=stdout:pattern=\n  \{(?s:.*)\n  \{
reject=stdout:contains=ipv6-unicast

# The whole view answers a record for each configured family, each tagged with its
# instance-id. Two tagged records and no third: no untagged or extra record either.
cmd=foreground:seq=4:exec=ze cli -c "show ospf ipv6 database | json":exit=0
expect=stdout:pattern="address-family": "ipv6-unicast",\s*"instance-id": 0,
expect=stdout:pattern="address-family": "ipv4-unicast",\s*"instance-id": 64,
reject=stdout:pattern=\n  \{(?s:.*)\n  \{(?s:.*)\n  \{

# The database record holds address-family, instance-id and lsas, so the header is the
# same whether the columns are declared or sorted by name: this proves the client shows
# the identity columns ahead of the record's LSAs, and the unit test
# TestOSPFv3ViewTableLeadsWithTheInstance proves the declared order on a route row.
cmd=foreground:seq=5:exec=ze cli -c "show ospf ipv6 database | table":exit=0
expect=stdout:contains=│ address-family │ instance-id │
expect=stdout:contains=ipv6-unicast
expect=stdout:contains=ipv4-unicast
